    routeSummary: "Delhi → Agra → Jaipur → Jaipur",
    intro: "Four days is the shortest Golden Triangle that does not feel like a race. The extra night goes to Jaipur, and that is exactly where it belongs: three days forces you to choose between Amber Fort and the walled-city core, and four days lets you have both without starting either at a jog.\n\nThe shape is Delhi one night, Agra one night, Jaipur two. You take the Gatimaan Express down to Agra in about 100 minutes, sleep there for the sunrise slot at the Taj, then drive to Jaipur through Fatehpur Sikri the next morning. From Jaipur you can fly out directly, or drive back to Delhi in five to six hours if your flight home is from there.\n\nThe fixed constraint is the same as on every version of this route: the Taj Mahal is closed every Friday. With four days you have slightly more room to shuffle, but the fix still has to happen at the booking stage, not on the ground. Decide which calendar day you are in Agra before you book a single hotel.",
    bestFor: "Travellers who want the full Golden Triangle including Amber Fort, and who would rather see three cities properly than four cities badly.",
    quickAnswer: "Four days is the Golden Triangle with Jaipur given a proper day: one night Delhi, one Agra, two Jaipur. The second Jaipur night is what buys you Amber Fort, which the three-day version has to drop. Delhi to Agra is 100 minutes on the Gatimaan Express. The Taj is closed every Friday, so plan the Agra day around it.",
