        narrative: "If you did not go at sunrise yesterday, you can still be at the Taj gate at opening and on the road by 09:00. Otherwise leave Agra around 08:00. Fatehpur Sikri is an hour west: Akbar's red sandstone capital, built from scratch in the 1570s and abandoned after fourteen years, so the whole complex survives without later rebuilding. Ninety minutes is enough; the Buland Darwaza and the Diwan-i-Khas with its single carved central pillar are the parts people remember.\n\nAnother three hours brings you into Jaipur in the early afternoon. Spend it in the old walled city, which is compact enough to do on foot: the City Palace, still partly the residence of the former royal family; Jantar Mantar, an eighteenth-century observatory of stone instruments built at architectural scale, including a sundial accurate to two seconds; and the Hawa Mahal, which is essentially a five-storey screen so the women of the court could watch the street unseen.\n\nEnd on the ridge at Nahargarh Fort for sunset over the whole grid of the city, then eat in the old town. Tomorrow is the fort day and it wants an early start.",
