        narrative: "Amber Fort is the reason the fourth day exists. Go at opening, around 08:00, both for the light on the hillside and because the courtyards fill fast by mid-morning. The fort is a hill palace of successive courtyards, and the Sheesh Mahal — mirrored so completely that a couple of candles once lit the room — is the piece people come for. Walk up rather than taking the elephant ride; the animals on that route have been a long-running welfare concern, and the climb is fifteen minutes.\n\nBelow the fort, the Panna Meena ka Kund stepwell and the Jal Mahal water palace on the lake are both a few minutes away and cost you nothing but time. Come back into the city by lunch.\n\nThe afternoon is where Jaipur repays a second day. The city has been a working craft centre for three hundred years — hand block printing, blue pottery, gem cutting and textile work — and spending two or three hours actually doing one of these is a better use of the time than adding a fourth fort. Alternatively, eat with a local family, or take the old city again after dark, when the bazaar around Johari and Bapu is at its best. If you are flying out on day 5, Jaipur airport is twenty minutes from the old city; if you are returning to Delhi by road, allow five to six hours and leave early.",
